@startuml
!include https://raw.githubusercontent.com/proveo-ca/identity/main/proveo.puml
skinparam noteTextAlignment left
' purpose: who reads the operator's terminal while an agent runs, and how the pumps end without losing the child's last line
' covers: internal/ptyproxy/ptyproxy.go
' covers: internal/ptyproxy/overlaytty.go
' covers: internal/ptyproxy/drain_test.go
' covers: internal/ptyproxy/ptyproxy_test.go
' covers: internal/backend/sandbox/sandbox.go (ptyproxy.Usable, ptyproxy.New)
' covers: internal/run/helpers.go
' covers: internal/backend/dockeregress/dockeregress.go (ExecAgentWithProxy)
'   what the input pump then FILTERS . _spec/internal/ptyproxy/terminal-report-filter.puml
'   what the output pump records ..... _spec/internal/runlog/run-transcript.puml
'   the modal this hands its screen to _spec/internal/choiceui/choice-prompt-render.puml
' Notes are capped at 5 lines x 50 columns; one idea per anchor.
'
' ── WHY THIS FILE EXISTS: REHOMED 2026-09-16 ───────────────────────────────
' Everything below was written down inside _spec/internal/reviewgate/pty-review-
' proxy.puml, which documented the docker review tier's interactive consent gate.
' sbx is the runtime under test now and that gate is retired with the mechanism
' (_spec/tests/testing-strategy.puml) — but `internal/ptyproxy` is NOT retired.
' internal/backend/sandbox/sandbox.go builds a `ptyproxy.New` on EVERY interactive
' sbx run, for the stdin report filter and the stdout trace, and internal/run/
' helpers.go builds one for the bare path. So the PTY mechanics outlive the gate
' that provoked them. They are kept here rather than deleted with it; the consent
' prompt, the unix-socket transport and the CONNECT hook are what died.
'
' ── THE ONE INVARIANT EVERYTHING ELSE DEFENDS ──────────────────────────────
' proveo holds the PTY master and hands the child the slave, so the proxy is the
' ONLY process reading the real terminal. A second reader is not an untidiness:
' the kernel hands each byte to whichever process gets there first, so keystrokes
' split nondeterministically between them and no amount of careful drawing fixes
' it. Every rule below is that invariant defended in one more place.
'
' Without a proxy — headless, or any run where `ptyproxy.Usable` is false because
' stdin or stdout is not a terminal — the child inherits the terminal directly and
' none of this applies.
'
' ── THE DRAIN BUG IS THE REASON `Run` HAS A WAITGROUP AT ALL ───────────────
' FIXED 2026-09-09. `pty.Start` returns with the child ALREADY RUNNING, and the
' output pump was started two statements later. A child that writes one line and
' exits is gone first, and `Run`'s deferred `m.Close()` then tore the master down
' with the output still inside it. MEASURED at a few percent of runs — and the run
' that loses its last line is the one that died on it, so the loss lands exactly
' where the transcript matters most.
'
' A read deadline is NOT the alternative fix, and it reads as the obvious one:
' `os.File.SetReadDeadline` returns nil on `/dev/ptmx` and is then ignored.
' MEASURED — a read blocked the full 30s until the child exited, deadline set and
' accepted. The master is also passed to the pump EXPLICITLY, because `p.master`
' is nil'd by the same defer that closes it and a pump scheduled late used to
' fetch the nil and copy nothing.
'
title The PTY proxy — one reader of the real terminal, and how its pumps end

actor "Operator" as OP

node "proveo — internal/ptyproxy" as PX <<host>> {
  component """Proxy.Run""\nowns the PTY master" as RUN <<app>>
  component "stdin pump\nreal tty → master" as IN <<app>>
  component "stdout pump\nmaster → real tty" as OUT <<app>>
  component """OverlayTty""\nhand-off channel · operator's tty" as OVER <<app>>
}

node "agent process" as CH <<host>> {
  component "PTY slave is its tty\n(sbx agent · docker agent · --shell)" as CHILD <<app>>
}

component "a SECOND reader\n""tcell.NewScreen"" · a bare prompt" as SECOND <<error>>
component "the child's last line\nlost to ""m.Close()""" as LOST <<error>>

OP ARROW_MAIN IN : keystrokes
IN ARROW_MAIN RUN
RUN ARROW_MAIN CHILD : slave handed to the child
CHILD ARROW_MAIN OUT
OUT ARROW_MAIN OP : rendered frames
IN ARROW_OPTIONAL OVER : re-routed while a modal holds the screen
OVER ARROW_MAIN OP
IN ARROW_ERROR SECOND : what the design exists to prevent
OUT ARROW_ERROR LOST : without the drain

note bottom of IN
  **One reader, start to finish.** The child holds
  the slave, so nothing else contends for stdin. A
  goroutine already blocked in ""read()"" consumes
  the next byte whatever a flag says, so the pump
  re-routes rather than standing down.
end note

note bottom of OVER
  **A modal is given bytes, never its own tty.**
  ""OverlayTty"" reads the pump's hand-off channel
  and writes the operator's terminal, which makes
  a full-screen prompt the exclusive consumer
  without a second reader ever existing.
end note

note bottom of SECOND
  **""tcell.NewScreen"" is the trap.** It opens
  ""/dev/tty"" and runs its own input loop, so the
  modal renders correctly and then takes no
  keystrokes — measured on a live run. Build over
  ""NewTerminfoScreenFromTty"" instead.
end note

note right of OUT
  **""Run"" does not return until this pump has
  drained the master.** ""pty.Start"" returns with
  the child ALREADY RUNNING, so a one-line child
  exits first and the deferred ""m.Close()"" tears
  the master down with the output still in it.
end note

note right of OUT
  **Only the OUT pump is waited on.** The child
  was the last holder of the slave, so the master
  read fails when it exits — a ""WaitGroup"" there
  costs nothing, bounded by ""drainGrace"" (2s)
  for a grandchild still holding the slave.
end note

note right of OUT
  **The IN pump cannot be waited on.** It blocks
  reading the operator's stdin, which never
  reaches EOF, so it can only notice the closed
  master on the next keystroke. Waiting on it
  would hang every exit.
end note

note bottom of LOST
  **A read deadline is a no-op, not a fix.**
  ""os.File.SetReadDeadline"" returns nil on
  ""/dev/ptmx"" and is then ignored: measured, a
  read blocked the full 30s until the child exited
  with the deadline set and accepted.
end note

note top of RUN
  **The terminal fd is captured once.** Calling
  ""In\.Fd()"" later races the pump's concurrent
  close, and forces the file back into blocking
  mode on every call. Raw mode is restored on
  every exit path, signals included.
end note

note top of CHILD
  **Forward the operator's real SIGWINCH.** The
  child's tty is the slave, so a window resize the
  operator makes reaches it only if the proxy
  passes it on; otherwise a TUI keeps rendering at
  the size it started with.
end note

note as Repaint #F4F9FB
  **Restore by forcing a repaint, never by
  replaying.** Anything that takes the screen from
  the child leaves it owning the alternate buffer,
  so buffered output is dropped and the child is
  made to repaint instead.
end note

note as RepaintHow #F4F9FB
  **The resize must be GENUINE.** Set the PTY to
  ""cols-1"" and back to ""cols"": both deliver
  SIGWINCH, and because the dimensions actually
  change, a TUI that early-outs on a no-op resize
  still does a full relayout. This is tmux's trick.
end note

note as RepaintLock #F4F9FB
  **A bare SIGWINCH at unchanged size is not
  reliable** — many TUIs skip it. The resize holds
  the lock for its whole duration, so a child that
  exits mid-overlay makes it a no-op instead of an
  operation on a dead fd.
end note
@enduml
